WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.162 --> 00:00:04.120
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hello.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:05.800
I'm Dustin Kirkland,
and I'm joined here

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.740
with Janet Kuo, software
engineer at Google,

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.230
and she's going to share with
us a bit of her experience

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:13.870
around Kubernetes
here at KubeCon

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:19.240
in Copenhagen. I understand that
working in the Workloads area

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:24.280
in Kubernetes, you've got some
expert perspectives on how

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.980
Kubernetes is changing
the life of an application

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:29.670
developer and an
IT administrator.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:30.970
JANET KUO: So yes.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:34.780
So Kubernetes
absolutely helps people

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:37.630
stop thinking about
infrastructure

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:41.530
or their computers,
machines, but start

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:44.350
thinking about the
things that they actually

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:49.990
care about like services and
how to run their applications

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:54.520
and also other
ecosystems such as Istio

00:00:54.520 --> 00:01:00.550
also help people to decouple
the operations and developers

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:06.280
and make it very easy for
them to work independently

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.274
but coordinate together.

00:01:08.274 --> 00:01:09.190
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah.

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:11.910
So you'd say that it's really
helping application developers

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.560
spend time on their
application, their code,

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.200
far less so than the rest
of the infrastructure site.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.280
JANET KUO: Yeah.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.520
And the operations
team can work on things

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.860
like how to deploy
the applications

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:28.420
and how to manage them,
how to manage the policies

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.080
of those applications.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:31.330
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:35.720
So from a workload perspective,
what types of workloads

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:40.480
are you working to
improve in Kubernetes?

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.920
JANET KUO: So when
I first started,

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.712
Kubernetes only supported
stateless workloads then.

00:01:47.712 --> 00:01:48.670
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:51.007
JANET KUO: So I wrote
the deployment API.

00:01:51.007 --> 00:01:51.840
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: OK.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.980
JANET KUO: But then we
figured that people need

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.540
more than stateless workloads.

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:58.780
So then we have the
StatefulSet that

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:00.990
helps people to run their
Stateful applications.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:02.030
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Sure.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:05.560
JANET KUO: And we also have
the jobs in CronJobs API

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:07.002
for batch processing.

00:02:07.002 --> 00:02:07.960
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:12.490
JANET KUO: And most recently,
we had the native support

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:14.267
for Kubernetes in Spark.

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:15.100
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: OK.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.832
JANET KUO: And that's just
announced a while ago.

00:02:17.832 --> 00:02:18.790
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:22.240
JANET KUO: And then if
people are using Spark,

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.702
they can run it on
Kubernetes Native now.

00:02:24.702 --> 00:02:25.660
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:28.931
Maybe for just giving
a bit of education

00:02:28.931 --> 00:02:30.430
to some of the
people watching this,

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:32.304
can you maybe explain
some of the differences

00:02:32.304 --> 00:02:36.670
between stateful, stateless,
and then batch processing,

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:38.530
and how that fits
in to Kubernetes?

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:39.280
JANET KUO: Sure.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.220
So for stateless
applications, it's

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.680
things like your
web applications

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:49.780
that you may run multiple
instance of the same thing,

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.930
and they're seen as disposable.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:56.950
So if something
dies, you don't care.

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:58.030
You just create more.

00:02:58.030 --> 00:02:58.988
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:02:58.988 --> 00:03:01.330
JANET KUO: Before
stateful applications,

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:03.792
you care about their
unique identity.

00:03:03.792 --> 00:03:04.750
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:08.230
JANET KUO: So each of
your pod, your instance--

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:11.140
your application
instance-- will be similar,

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:14.810
but they'll each have their
own identity, their storage,

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:16.270
their network ID.

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:16.430
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:17.596
JANET KUO: Things like that.

00:03:17.596 --> 00:03:19.960
And for batch
processing, it's for you

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:27.220
to be able to tell Kubernetes
how many pods I want to run

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:29.452
at a time to process my jobs.

00:03:29.452 --> 00:03:30.410
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:31.090
Yeah.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:33.820
So maybe stateful applications
might be something

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:35.440
like a database, for instance?

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.127
JANET KUO: Yeah, a database
or a clustered application.

00:03:38.127 --> 00:03:39.710
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Cluster applications.

00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:40.210
OK.

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:43.280
Stateless might be
something like a web front

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:44.054
end or a proxy.

00:03:44.054 --> 00:03:44.720
JANET KUO: Yeah.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:45.210
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right?

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:45.850
JANET KUO: Yes.

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:48.700
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And then batch
processing might be image--

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:51.070
JANET KUO: Yeah, image
processing or you

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:53.600
have a bunch of emails
you want to send

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:54.976
or you want to
back up something.

00:03:54.976 --> 00:03:57.308
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And do you
feel like all three of those

00:03:57.308 --> 00:04:00.550
are equally as well supported
as Workloads in the Kubernetes

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:02.080
community today?

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.680
JANET KUO: So the stateless
applications are definitely

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:06.822
the most mature ones.

00:04:06.822 --> 00:04:07.780
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:08.640
Because that's where we started.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:09.306
JANET KUO: Yeah.

00:04:09.306 --> 00:04:15.380
That's where we started, and
that's the simplest model

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:17.120
that we support.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:21.899
And for the other, like,
badge and stateful workflows,

00:04:21.899 --> 00:04:24.220
they're still--

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:28.330
it's also pretty mature
because they're OGA.

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:32.710
But we keep hearing
people's feedback,

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:35.140
and we are trying
to improve those.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:37.630
For example, for
batch, some people

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:42.550
want index job, like
they want better support.

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:46.240
And we may support
that in core or we

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:50.920
may create something, some
customized controllers

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:54.370
for people to be able
to do their own things

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:55.420
outside of core.

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:56.500
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.960
And from a Workload
perspective, what do you

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:02.630
think are maybe some of the
most interesting CNCF and Cloud

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:06.970
Native ecosystem projects today?

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:09.770
JANET KUO: So there are
a bunch of projects.

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:12.390
For example, the
Istio is one of the--

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:13.790
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Istio, yeah.

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:14.456
JANET KUO: Yeah.

00:05:14.456 --> 00:05:16.130
CNCF project for me.

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:21.220
And also a lot of
people around Workloads,

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:25.130
they are building
different tools

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:31.220
for making it easy for you
to write your controllers.

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:32.570
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:35.002
JANET KUO: Like operators--

00:05:35.002 --> 00:05:35.960
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:40.640
JANET KUO: --or like
Kubebuilder, things like that.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.270
And that's very
interesting for me.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:44.656
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah,
that is really interesting.

00:05:44.656 --> 00:05:47.030
So you're involved with the
community quite a bit, right?

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:49.730
Would you say most of
your work is done--

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:52.120
most of your engineering
work is done in the public,

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:55.034
in the Kubernetes
community at this point?

00:05:55.034 --> 00:05:55.700
JANET KUO: Yeah.

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:58.444
So most of my work
lives on GitHub.

00:05:58.444 --> 00:05:59.360
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:00.026
JANET KUO: Yeah.

00:06:00.026 --> 00:06:01.100
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah.

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:02.840
And what's been your
experience so far

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:05.480
working in the
Kubernetes community?

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:07.990
JANET KUO: So the
community has grown a lot.

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:14.840
And at first, we were pushing
to find more and more people

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:15.710
to write code.

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:21.200
But now we find that we
have so many PRs to review,

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:23.940
and we are short on reviewers.

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:30.470
So we are trying to fix that by
ramping up more contributors,

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:34.490
make them own more
code, and be one

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:38.002
of the reviewers of
some expert areas.

00:06:38.002 --> 00:06:38.960
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:39.380
JANET KUO: Yeah.

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:41.088
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And
reviewing code still

00:06:41.088 --> 00:06:42.840
takes a developer to do.

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:44.450
Are there other areas
of the community

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:48.920
that maybe non-developers can
get involved from a Kubernetes

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:49.430
perspective?

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:50.390
JANET KUO: Sure.

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:56.102
So for example, docs
is one of the most--

00:06:56.102 --> 00:06:57.310
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Appreciated.

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:59.660
JANET KUO: --appreciated
work, yeah.

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:05.420
So I remember when I joined,
where we don't have--

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:08.240
so people often
criticize, like, oh,

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:10.070
Kubernetes doesn't
have good docs,

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:14.000
or we cannot understand
the docs that you write.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.450
So we've been improving
that, and we have created

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:20.300
more and more tutorials.

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:23.900
And we recently
did a doc revamp.

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:26.690
So if you go to the
Kubernetes style website,

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:28.670
you'll see the new format.

00:07:28.670 --> 00:07:30.980
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Kubernetes.o,
yeah, and documentation.

00:07:30.980 --> 00:07:31.480
Yeah.

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:32.021
That's great.

00:07:32.021 --> 00:07:33.344
Testing QA, of course.

00:07:33.344 --> 00:07:34.010
JANET KUO: Yeah.

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:34.295
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right?

00:07:34.295 --> 00:07:35.211
JANET KUO: Testing QA.

00:07:35.211 --> 00:07:37.640
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: I'm involved
in SIG product actually.

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:40.130
So there's a SIG PM, a
product management one.

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:41.570
So, yeah, there's
quite a few ways

00:07:41.570 --> 00:07:44.410
to contribute to Kubernetes
outside of just writing

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:45.410
and reading code, right?

00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:46.480
JANET KUO: Yeah.

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:47.930
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Good.

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:49.730
Along those lines, do
you have any advice

00:07:49.730 --> 00:07:52.670
for any students
or young engineers

00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:54.680
who are coming to the
Kubernetes community

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:56.930
perhaps for the first time?

00:07:56.930 --> 00:08:00.200
JANET KUO: So for
students, I would--

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:06.080
so we have one contributor
who is called Lucas.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:09.920
He was a high school student
and was the youngest contributor

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:11.690
in our community.

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:14.480
So I want to call
out and to let people

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:19.051
know that don't feel intimidated
to join the Kubernetes

00:08:19.051 --> 00:08:19.550
community.

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:20.915
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: It's a
very welcoming community.

00:08:20.915 --> 00:08:22.610
JANET KUO: Yeah,
it's very welcoming,

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:24.890
and if you don't
know where to start,

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:29.210
you can just start with browsing
all the SIGs and to figure out

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:31.500
which one you want to join.

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:34.130
And if you don't know
which one to join,

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:37.340
you can just join the
Contributor Experience,

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:40.730
because that's the first
you can get feedback,

00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:45.890
like what I see from the
contributor side, what

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:47.002
process you can improve.

00:08:47.002 --> 00:08:47.960
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:48.760
JANET KUO: Yeah.

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:49.926
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: It's great.

00:08:49.926 --> 00:08:51.440
You called out Lucas.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:53.780
Is there anyone else in
the Kubernetes community

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:55.970
that you find particularly
inspiring, someone

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:58.960
that you might want to
give a shout-out to,

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:01.430
a public thank you?

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:03.890
JANET KUO: Oh, there
are so many people.

00:09:03.890 --> 00:09:05.930
For example, Brian Grant.

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:08.570
I first worked with him.

00:09:08.570 --> 00:09:10.820
And then Eric [INAUDIBLE].

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:12.530
And for people
outside of Google,

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:17.990
I would say Clayton
is one of the earliest

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:19.760
contributors to Kubernetes.

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:25.210
And he's been involved in every
conversation and every PR,

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:27.432
and he has a lot of insights.

00:09:27.432 --> 00:09:28.390
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:29.150
JANET KUO: Yeah.

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:32.960
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And
one final question--

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:35.930
how did you first
come to Kubernetes?

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:38.900
What was your introduction
to Kubernetes like?

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:40.550
JANET KUO: So I came to--

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:43.980
so Kubernetes is my
first project at Google.

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:47.630
And when I
interviewed at Google,

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:50.570
I got a phone call
from Team Hawking,

00:09:50.570 --> 00:09:54.290
and he just basically
sold me the idea.

00:09:54.290 --> 00:09:57.897
Because Kubernetes wasn't
a thing at that time yet.

00:09:57.897 --> 00:09:58.730
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: OK.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:02.420
JANET KUO: And a lot of
other orchestrating solutions

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:04.690
are much more popular
than Kubernetes,

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:07.510
and people even don't know
how to spell or pronounce

00:10:07.510 --> 00:10:09.920
Kubernetes at that time.

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:11.170
So yeah.

00:10:11.170 --> 00:10:13.740
After getting his phone
call, I was like, I'm in.

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:14.740
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: I'm so.

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:15.020
JANET KUO: Yeah.

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:15.625
I'm sold.

00:10:15.625 --> 00:10:16.750
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Very cool.

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:18.400
Well, Janet, thank you
very much for your time.

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:19.066
JANET KUO: Yeah.

00:10:19.066 --> 00:10:20.640
Thank you.

